The training medians show that inverted MAPbI3 cells reach 18–21 % only when three conditions are met simultaneously: (i) a high-work-function, low-recombination HTL (PTAA, P3CT-Na or Li/Mg-doped NiO), (ii) a fullerene ETL completed by a thin hole-blocking/contact layer (BCP, Bphen or PEI), and (iii) a chemically compatible cathode (Cu, Ag or Al). PEDOT:PSS, undoped oxides, missing blockers and exotic back-contact stacks systematically sit 3–8 points lower, consistent with the well-known Voc deficit, acidity and ion-migration problems of those contacts.

Applying the same filters to the 100 unmeasured stacks immediately isolates five candidates whose layer sequences are essentially identical to the highest training recipes:

- c038 adds only a PEI dipole to the 20.0 % Au-np/NiO cell (improved cathode alignment);
- c030 is the 19.48 % PTAA/PMMA/Bphen device plus an extra electron-cascade layer;
- c097 is the 18.49 % P3CT-Na/C60/BCP cell with a chemically equivalent selenophene analogue;
- c041 replaces the TiO2 interlayer of the 18.18 % NiMgLiO cell by the more selective BCP;
- c044 is the canonical PTAA/PCBM/BCP/Cu stack that already yields 18.8 % when a PFN wetting layer is present.

All other pool entries either revert to PEDOT:PSS, omit the blocker, use undoped NiO, or employ scalable but lower-quality coatings (doctor-blading, slot-die on plastic) and are therefore expected to lie below 18 %.

Final: c038, c030, c097, c041, c044
